---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A single black line branching into a dozen diverging lines against a warm cream ground, each branch dated 1844 onward"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every week you'll work through one stage of a single fracture cascade:
    Joseph Smith's death in 1844 with no named successor, and the dozens of
    competing churches that argument produced. You'll read primary-source-depth
    accounts of contested events, apply a four-part framework — succession
    vacuum, charismatic claim, doctrinal pivot, institutional response — to a
    splinter group of your own choosing, and finish the semester having built a
    map of the whole movement's family tree. You walk out with two pieces of
    written argument (Assignment 1) and analysis (the final project), both
    grounded in evidence you can point to.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone interested in how institutions actually split — not just this one
    religious movement, but the general mechanics of succession crises,
    competing claims to legitimacy, and doctrine bending to justify a decision
    already made. No prior knowledge of Latter Day Saint history is assumed;
    what's assumed is a willingness to read primary sources closely and argue
    from evidence rather than from what "everybody knows" about a group you've
    likely only heard caricatured.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>
